<script module>
  import Panel from "$ui/components/Panel.abide"
  import benchFrontend from "$server/rpc/benchFrontend"

  // Format a nanosecond mean into the tightest readable unit — mirrors the CLI bench's `fmtNs`.
  function fmtNs(ns) {
    if (ns >= 1e6) return (ns / 1e6).toFixed(2) + " ms"
    if (ns >= 1e3) return (ns / 1e3).toFixed(2) + " µs"
    return ns.toFixed(0) + " ns"
  }

  // abide ÷ vanilla. A `†` marks a row measured close to the timing loop's own cost, where the ratio
  // understates the real multiple (the same footnote the CLI bench prints).
  function fmtRatio(row) {
    if (row.ratio === null) return "—"
    return row.ratio.toFixed(2) + "×" + (row.nearFloor ? " †" : "")
  }
</script>

<main class="page">
  <h1>Frontend render bench (live)</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    The same corpus the CLI <code>bun run bench</code> measures, run <em>here, on this request</em>. A
    streaming <code>GET</code> compiles each <code>.abide</code> template with the AOT emitter and times
    its SSR <code>render</code> path, then <code>jsonl</code> streams one row per scenario — so the table
    below fills in live via <code>&#123;#for await&#125;</code>. Hit refresh to re-run the whole corpus.
  </p>
  <Panel id="the-corpus-timed-on-this-request" title="The corpus, timed on this request">
    <button
      data-testid="rerun"
      class="mb-4 rounded bg-slate-800 px-3 py-1.5 text-white"
      onclick={() => benchFrontend.refresh()}
    >
      Re-run bench
    </button>
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm" data-testid="bench-table">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-slate-300 text-left">
          <th class="py-1.5 pr-4">scenario</th>
          <th class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right whitespace-nowrap">render (ns/op)</th>
          <th class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right whitespace-nowrap">vanilla JS</th>
          <th class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right whitespace-nowrap">×</th>
          <th class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right whitespace-nowrap">ns/row</th>
          <th class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right whitespace-nowrap">iters</th>
          <th class="py-1.5">hand-written as</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {#for await row of benchFrontend()}
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-100" data-testid="bench-row">
            <td class="py-1.5 pr-4 font-mono">{row.name}</td>
            <td class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right whitespace-nowrap font-mono">{fmtNs(row.nsPerOp)}</td>
            <td class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right whitespace-nowrap font-mono text-slate-500">{row.vanillaNsPerOp === null ? "—" : fmtNs(row.vanillaNsPerOp)}</td>
            <td class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right whitespace-nowrap font-mono font-semibold">{fmtRatio(row)}</td>
            <td class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right whitespace-nowrap font-mono text-slate-500">{row.nsPerRow === null ? "—" : fmtNs(row.nsPerRow)}</td>
            <td class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right whitespace-nowrap font-mono text-slate-400" data-testid="bench-iters">{row.iters}</td>
            <td class="py-1.5 font-mono text-slate-500">{row.vanillaNote ?? "—"}</td>
          </tr>
        {:catch}
          <tr><td colspan="7" class="py-2 text-red-600">bench failed</td></tr>
        {/for}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Panel>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p class="lead">
      This measures <code>render</code> only: the pure server-side string build, which is genuinely
      abide-bound. Notice the <strong>ns/row</strong> column holds roughly flat as the list grows
      100&nbsp;→&nbsp;1000&nbsp;→&nbsp;10000 — render is O(n) in element count. (Client <code>mount</code>
      is excluded here: in a JS DOM its large-list cost is dominated by the DOM backend's O(n)
      <code>insertBefore</code>, not by abide.)
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      Every scenario is timed <strong>twice</strong>: once through abide, once through a hand-written,
      framework-free equivalent that produces the same markup (the shared
      <code>@abide/bench/vanillaBaselines</code> corpus — string concatenation, a plain loop, a ternary).
      The <strong>×</strong> column is abide ÷ vanilla: what the framework costs over doing it by hand. It
      is the hardware-neutral figure — the ns columns describe whatever machine this server runs on, the
      ratio does not.
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      Want the mount numbers anyway? The <a href="/platform/bench/client">in-browser mount bench</a> runs
      the same corpus through the real <code>mount</code> path, timed live in your own tab.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-500">
      † the op is close enough to the timing loop's own per-iteration cost that the constant — added to both
      sides — squashes the ratio toward 1.00×; the real multiple is higher.
    </p>
  </aside>
</main>
